 sieve inequality in non-abelian settings. Assuming the non-abelian large sieve inequality, we provide a proof of Artin's primitive root conjecture. Further, using duality techniques, we derive unconditional results towards the conjectured non-abelian large sieve inequality.\n\nView Paper\n\n7 views\n\nv1\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{murty2026nonabelianlargesieveartins, title={A non-abelian large sieve and Artin's primitive root conjecture}, author={M. Ram Murty and Sunil Naik}, year={2026}, eprint={2608.21573}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.NT}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.21573}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAbout | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/about)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; MathDB is a collaboration between Caltech mathematics faculty, graduate students and undergraduates, building an open database of the unsolved problems and conjectures of research mathematics: what each one says, what is known about it, and who has attempted it.\n\n# About MathDB\n\nMathDB is a collaboration between Caltech mathematics faculty, graduate students and undergraduates, building an open database of the unsolved problems and conjectures of research mathematics: what each one says, what is known about it, and who has attempted it.\n\nA Caltech collaboration\n\nProblems catalogued\n    88,148\n\nWith a recorded AI attempt\n    235\n\nClaimed solved with AI\n    201\n\n## Why now\n\nIn the past few months AI has started producing results in pure mathematics that working mathematicians take seriously.\n\n  * Riemann hypothesis: Claude Fable 5 improved a bound on the problem, open since 1859.\n  * Jacobian conjecture: a claimed counterexample settles it negatively in three or more dimensions, leaving the plane case open.\n  * Erdős unit-distance conjecture: disproved in 2026 by a construction packing more unit distances into the plane than the conjecture allowed, credited to an internal Ope